WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.347
Deterministic ordering matters when you use ROW_NUMBER.

00:00:03.347 --> 00:00:09.542
If two employees have equal salary, salary alone does not define which comes first.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:13.182
Adding employee ID creates a stable order.

00:00:13.182 --> 00:00:16.792
But be careful with RANK and DENSE_RANK.

00:00:16.792 --> 00:00:24.072
If you include employee ID inside the ranking ORDER BY, the tied salary rows are no longer peers.

00:00:24.072 --> 00:00:27.062
Choose the ordering based on the meaning you want.

00:00:27.062 --> 00:00:33.282
Connect this idea to a sales team calculating running revenue and top products by region.

00:00:33.282 --> 00:00:39.232
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:39.232 --> 00:00:47.047
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:52.902
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:52.902 --> 00:00:58.684
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
